#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317677 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccdd6eb6-30fa-580d-92a3-888c20d92865" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{64-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317679} {:answer/id #uuid "e2368140-39a2-5d26-a5a7-5c4c57975e4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=-\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317680} {:answer/id #uuid "c5ea363a-f4d3-54d3-bc2a-9ffbd48302c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317681} {:answer/id #uuid "0184e5c6-1bf0-590c-9ece-2e819ba361e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317682} {:answer/id #uuid "9234e108-cc9b-5b79-a281-a9e3e1a74665" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317683}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2368140-39a2-5d26-a5a7-5c4c57975e4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=-\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317680} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc59459f-1e90-54cd-84d9-f3f11e7a1a71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b60038c6-cb0f-5d07-b76f-fbce9c0d51b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-122339" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of decreasing $x$ where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $\\frac{1}{16}x\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $0\\,m$ when its velocity is $-2\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/assets/ba6a85da04d9ba03bcdba9b938dac208a770933310cd25c19bf0ec3e19ef17c7.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $\\frac{1}{16}x\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{16}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=\\frac{1}{16}x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (\\frac{1}{16}x)\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=0\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=-2\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(-2)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{32}(0)^{2}+C \\\\ 2 & =0+C \\\\ C & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}+2 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{2}+4 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16}({x}^{2}+64) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)<0,$ so we can discard the positive square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=-\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+64}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317691 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29e007c7-6cc3-5e98-99e9-dc4b2cdd07ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317693} {:answer/id #uuid "64345185-5abe-5170-9e41-b6e3f42d7d11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317694} {:answer/id #uuid "fe150ef1-3540-5259-be8b-b205591045c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317695} {:answer/id #uuid "3db94eb2-4153-5552-b898-d5c7e4d753b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317696} {:answer/id #uuid "9dae4abf-bd8d-5985-8531-89977efe3c30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64345185-5abe-5170-9e41-b6e3f42d7d11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317694} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b2f47d5-7302-5cb4-88e6-fef841d5bf39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7832c005-e5d4-5bbe-91b5-55956ce35ed8" :question/math-academy-id "q-122342" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x,$ away from a fixed origin $O.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $(4{x}^{3}+4x)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $v=\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=2\\,\\text{m}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/assets/4e0b8b816ca3b86fa90ea785df1308f53a4763baa2c2aa2192b9d7bfa0259855.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(4{x}^{3}+4x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4{x}^{3}+4x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4{x}^{3}+4x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int (4{x}^{3}+4x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{6})^{2} & ={1}^{4}+2(1)^{2}+C \\\\ 3 & =3+C \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{4}+2{x}^{2} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=\\sqrt{2({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2})}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\sqrt{2({2}^{4}+2(2)^{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{48} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337972 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "187225fc-7988-5d96-aea7-2f30a1ee94d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.1\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337974} {:answer/id #uuid "dc99599f-4d8d-5ab0-8bc0-100bc41f2125" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.1\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337975} {:answer/id #uuid "3d2b5695-0d1f-5150-9e9a-8e454b3d1f1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.9\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337976} {:answer/id #uuid "2d1d39c1-0fa5-542c-bd94-26d7c12c5ae8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.6\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337977} {:answer/id #uuid "391c2479-5be2-5ba6-b72b-8c3343494262" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.7\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337978}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc99599f-4d8d-5ab0-8bc0-100bc41f2125" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.1\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337975} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9f08078-9f75-5dd2-81e7-8f8e77a36640" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "036ad9a6-f37b-58f5-84a3-04376ec252d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-122348" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of decreasing $x$ where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$ If $v=-\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=3\\,\\text{m}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/assets/3278eca2ae91f59c7c20d91f9190611291924dcc0cd6ffe74456552ba8400335.png)\n\nSince the magnitude of the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1})\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=-2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=-\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(-\\sqrt{\\pi })^{2} & =-2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1+C \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{2} & =-2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}+C \\\\ C & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\pi -2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2(\\pi -2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2(\\pi -2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)<0,$ so we can discard the positive square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=-\\sqrt{2(\\pi -2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =-\\sqrt{2(\\pi -2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡3)} \\\\  & ≈-1.1\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337979 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "550bb33b-c427-5667-977d-56ff8c6b458e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Displacement of a Particle as a Function of Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27fdafa4-f068-570e-a0c9-6545f8fdefe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=(3t+1)^{4}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337981} {:answer/id #uuid "053297e4-bfe0-5298-aa3a-d3833e327560" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=(3t+1)^{4/3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337982} {:answer/id #uuid "048b1522-739c-563d-8e9b-4df9a9f1017a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=(4t+1)^{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337983} {:answer/id #uuid "99447f2d-715c-5ec3-91a6-d4ddb02cf267" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=(t+2)^{2/3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337984} {:answer/id #uuid "77a32913-8644-5b34-b32c-5adab56c69ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=(t+2)^{4/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "053297e4-bfe0-5298-aa3a-d3833e327560" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=(3t+1)^{4/3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337982} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ef253d5-3b28-532f-a890-10eb1ea362cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "528f1204-b150-5ae3-b3f2-1df94a35f90d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122351" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ its acceleration is $\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x+1}}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O$ and has velocity $v=4\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/assets/ee516744d915553bf962b498af522cf0006d42c5d0e0e2a003e6f385dd069e4a.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x+1}}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x+1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x+1}}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x+1}}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8\\sqrt{x+1}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=0\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=4\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8\\sqrt{x+1}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =8\\sqrt{0+1}+K \\\\ 8 & =8+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8\\sqrt{x+1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =16\\sqrt{x+1} \\\\ v & =\\pm 4\\sqrt[4]{x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=4\\sqrt[4]{x+1}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=4\\sqrt[4]{x+1}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\sqrt[4]{x+1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{x+1}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{x+1}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}(x+1)^{3/4} & =4t+{C}_{1} \\\\ (x+1)^{3/4} & =3t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=\\frac{3{C}_{1}}{4}.$ \n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n(x+1)^{3/4}=3t+1.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{3/4} & =3t+1 \\\\ x+1 & =(3t+1)^{4/3} \\\\ x & =(3t+1)^{4/3}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "550bb33b-c427-5667-977d-56ff8c6b458e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Displacement of a Particle as a Function of Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2e3e716-1710-5481-8a72-2ec21016a4d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337988} {:answer/id #uuid "b902057a-5b5f-5568-b23a-12fe17c31c35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337989} {:answer/id #uuid "76473cd3-17be-57fb-a2e5-b5c499b89ecb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337990} {:answer/id #uuid "3bd9c68b-0aa3-51d6-bbf3-39f6e524172f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337991} {:answer/id #uuid "5ae7e5ad-bce8-5d0d-923e-1d2047544385" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2{e}^{-2t}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bd9c68b-0aa3-51d6-bbf3-39f6e524172f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337991} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa28606c-9a95-5d49-ab6c-088149f4d349" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "81bbd2d5-38c0-5ad0-acb6-520952814c74" :question/math-academy-id "q-122352" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $8{e}^{1-x}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed toward $O.$ Initially, $P$ is at $x=1\\,m$ and has velocity $v=4\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059489/assets/f08c9d1e8dce3c0ae7c37719cadc3fc60bf5de3030ce8c44ca7528a98f3abf7e.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $8{e}^{1-x}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-8{e}^{1-x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-8{e}^{1-x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -8{e}^{1-x}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8{e}^{1-x}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=4\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8{e}^{1-x}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =8{e}^{0}+K \\\\ 8 & =8+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8{e}^{1-x} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =16{e}^{1-x} \\\\ v & =\\pm 4{e}^{(1-x)/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=4{e}^{(1-x)/2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=4{e}^{(1-x)/2}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4{e}^{(1-x)/2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{e}^{(1-x)/2}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4 \\\\ \\int {e}^{(x-1)/2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int {e}^{(x-1)/2}\\text{d}x & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ 2{e}^{(x-1)/2} & =4t+{C}_{1} \\\\ {e}^{(x-1)/2} & =2t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=\\frac{{C}_{1}}{2}.$ \n\nWe're given that $x=1$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{e}^{(x-1)/2}=2t+1.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{(x-1)/2} & =2t+1 \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1) \\\\ x-1 & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1) \\\\ x & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
